1|Treasure Ticks: one inch parasites drawn to the smell of valuable metals and gems. They are chameleonic and change shape to mimic coins, gemstones, etc, where they hibernate until a warm blooded victim becomes available. If collected in a pouch with coins, the bugs burrow out in [[1d6]] minutes, entering the flesh of their victim to feed (they secrete a leech like anaesthesia). Detecting a burrowing tick requires a Perc check at disadvantage. If undetected, the tick causes blood poisoning ([[1d3]] Str and Dex loss after [[1d4]] hours, and a Madness). The effects do not subside until the bugs are removed, which requires surgery (Int (Apothecary*) check, causing [[1d4]] damage). If unsuccessful the victim also suffers Internal Bleeding.
2|Flesh Grubs: filthy two inch burrowing worms with wicked beaks. They consume corpses from the inside, kept warm by the foul gases they emit as they lay eggs and multiply. Infested corpses bloat as the fumes build, rendering them liable to explode (an Int (Apothecary*/Wilderness Lore*) check might notice the tell tale “flesh grubs” odour). If disturbed, the corpse ruptures, showering Melee range with grubs, which burrow for the heart. A Luck (Con) save is required to avoid death in [[1d4]] rounds. Fire or acid kills the grubs but causes the victim [[2d6]] damage.
3|Throat Leeches: fast swimming four inch marine parasites that lurk in water holes, rivers, and oceans. A PC swimming in an infested area requires a Luck save to avoid swallowing the horrible wrigglers. On a failed save, several leeches have entered the victim’s throat and latched onto their gullet. Over the next [[1d4]] hours, and each day afterwards, the leeches feed, causing 1 Str or Con loss (even chance). Removing the leeches requires surgery or alchemical tinctures that are poisonous if not properly administered. An Int (Apothecary*) check kills the parasites in [[1d3]] days. If failed, the victim also suffers [[2d4]] damage. A desperate victim might drink and regurgitate a flask of acid to kill the worms in minutes (the drinker suffers 3d6 damage, and cannot use their vocal chords for [[1d4]] months).